    "body": "I'd like some clarification on the original test in this thread, please.\r\n\r\nPer 'the unimplemented step', was it expected that these particular elements would expose a `title=\"\"` in the DOM viewer if no `title` was manually set? Is that why it was deemed that none implemented the step?\r\n\r\nIn the following example, I could see why this would not be ideal: ([codepen link](https://codepen.io/scottohara/pen/aqoyWG)) \r\n```\r\n<a title=\"Hypertext Markup Language\" href=\"#!\">\r\n    <abbr title=\"\">HTML</abbr>\r\n</a>\r\n```\r\nThe tooltip for the `a` can not be accessed by mouse hover due to the `abbr title=\"\"` \"blocking\" the `a` from the mouse cursor.\r\n\r\nTested with \r\n- Firefox 57.04 on Mac, ESR on PC\r\n- Chrome latest on Mac/PC\r\n- IE11 / Latest Edge\r\n\r\nSafari 11.0.2 on macOS 10.13.2 did reveal the `a` tooltip on mouse hover, unlike the other browsers.\r\n\r\nIE11 & Edge did reveal the `a` tooltip when the `a` received keyboard focus. (the only browsers that reveal a `title` on focusable content.) But this is understandable since it's the actual `a` that is focused, and the `abbr` can't \"get in the way\" of that, like it can with mouse hover.\r\n\r\nSimilar behavior when modifying the child elements of the `div` in the original test.  If the child elements of the `div` have a `title=\"\"`, the `div`'s tooltip can not be accessed by mouse hover, unless one hovers where there are no child nodes \"blocking\" the mouse cursor from the `div`.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n@domenic yes, screen readers largely do expose the information presented in title attributes.  [`link` and `style` titles are exposed in some browsers](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Alternative_style_sheets) (firefox and IE, maybe edge?) that allow for alternate style sheet switching via browser settings/options.\r\n\r\nI am not aware of screen readers surfacing the `title` on the `dfn` element.  \r\nJAWS allows for the `title` value of `abbr` or the obsolete `acronym` to be announced via a setting.\r\nI have not run any tests for `menuitem` since its unfortunately been deprecated.\r\n\r\n@annevk.  \r\n1. Thanks for reading. :)  \r\n2. Just to clarify, I was meaning to suggest that the attribute is only useful in limited circumstances per the UX issues I outlined in the article. \r\n\r\n\r\nHope this was useful.  Thank you ",
